finding: 1. src/extras/multiplayer/prediction.ts:287-289 rejects any post-join confirmation ahead of the client's simulated head, even when there is no rollback to perform. Independently reproduced with two Worlds, an empty schema and the seeded byte link: confirm tick 0, advance only the server to tick 1, send its frame, flush the client => Error: Missing prediction tick 1. The catch-up path at line 289 is restricted to the first image. A slow client, a server tick arriving just before its local tick, or the in-tab host stepping server first can therefore fail before stage 8's pacing can recover. Current exactness tests maintain a roughly ten-tick client lead and never exercise this case. Accept an ahead-of-head confirmation by advancing/catching up safely or resynchronizing rather than requiring a nonexistent past snapshot. 2. src/extras/multiplayer/prediction.ts:190-195 turns a normal confirmation stall into a gameplay exception with no bounded waiting/resync state. Independently reproduced with PredictionPlugin and default ClientPrediction: 33 ordinary step(1/60) calls without incoming confirmations throw at tick 33 (clock has already advanced). This is 550 ms from construction, or about 383 ms of additional outage with the witness's ten-tick lead. WebSocket head-of-line stalls and foreground/background timing can reach this in ordinary play. The bound is useful, but should stop prediction/catch up or request an image without throwing through the game's frame loop. The existing bound test specifically expects the throw and so cannot prove usable behavior under the stage 8 stall/slow-client cases. Raising the limit only postpones the failure.
